  <section class="section section--soft" data-nav-label="Problem" id="problem">
  <div class="page-shell">
  <div class="section-heading-split">
  <div data-reveal=""><p class="eyebrow">The multi-store problem</p><h2 class="section-title">More company stores should not mean more tabs, more spreadsheets, and more people.</h2></div>
  <p class="section-copy" data-reveal="">Large distributors rarely struggle to launch the next Shopify store. The pain comes later, when the operations team has to keep opening store after store to find orders, build reports, split supplier work, create POs, and answer enterprise client requests.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="problem-grid">
  <article class="problem-card" data-reveal=""><span class="problem-card__number">01</span><h3>You cannot see the whole business at once.</h3><p>Orders and performance are spread across separate Shopify admins, so getting a clear portfolio view means logging into stores individually or rebuilding the same report outside Shopify.</p></article>
  <article class="problem-card" data-reveal=""><span class="problem-card__number">02</span><h3>Your team is doing work the order already told you to do.</h3><p>People still split orders by supplier, create purchase orders, enter supplier orders, decide the decorator path, and chase the next step manually.</p></article>
  <article class="problem-card" data-reveal=""><span class="problem-card__number">03</span><h3>Every large client asks for something different.</h3><p>One needs PunchOut. Another needs a supplier connection. Another has a unique routing or approval rule. Off-the-shelf apps stop fitting once the workflow gets specific.</p></article>
  </div>
  <div class="better-approach-type" data-reveal="">
  <p class="eyebrow">The better approach</p>
  <h3 class="better-approach-type__interactive"><span class="better-approach-type__soft">More stores. More tabs. More manual work.</span><span>Bring every company store into one place.</span><span class="better-approach-type__accent">Automate the repetitive work eating your time and margins.</span></h3>
  <div aria-label="Recommended approach" class="better-approach-type__steps"><span>Centralize</span><i></i><span>Connect</span><i></i><span>Automate</span></div>
  </div>
  </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--gray" data-nav-label="Base Platform" id="platform">
  <div class="page-shell platform-layout">
  <div class="platform-sticky" data-reveal=""><p class="eyebrow">Start with the base platform</p><h2 class="section-title section-title--small">One dashboard for every company store.</h2><p class="section-copy">If your biggest problem is simply managing many stores, start here. Bring your Shopify company stores into one control layer so your team can see the portfolio without living inside separate Shopify admins.</p><a class="text-link" href="#architecture">Then add only what you need <span>→</span></a></div>
  <div class="platform-list">
  <article class="platform-item" data-reveal=""><span class="platform-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 5h16v14H4zM8 9h8M8 13h5"></path></svg></span><div><h3>See orders from every store in one place</h3><p>Give operations one central order view across multiple client programs instead of making people jump between Shopify stores just to see what came in.</p></div></article>
  <article class="platform-item" data-reveal=""><span class="platform-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 18V9M10 18V5M16 18v-7M22 18V7"></path></svg></span><div><h3>Get portfolio-level reporting</h3><p>See reporting across the company store portfolio, then drill into individual stores when a client, account manager, or operations team needs the detail.</p></div></article>
  <article class="platform-item" data-reveal=""><span class="platform-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><circle cx="6" cy="12" r="2"></circle><circle cx="18" cy="6" r="2"></circle><circle cx="18" cy="18" r="2"></circle><path d="m8 11 8-4M8 13l8 4"></path></svg></span><div><h3>Manage different store types together</h3><p>Your portfolio can include simple Shopify company stores and SAGE-powered Shopify stores. The platform gives you a common operational view while each store keeps the setup it needs.</p></div></article>
  <article class="platform-item" data-reveal=""><span class="platform-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 6h14v12H5z"></path><path d="m8 12 3 3 5-6"></path></svg></span><div><h3>Stop at the platform if that solves the problem</h3><p>You do not have to buy a full automation project. Use the base platform for centralized management, then add workflow automation only when the operational return makes sense.</p></div></article>
  </div>
  </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section solution-package-section" data-nav-label="Platform + Add-ons" id="architecture">
